<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Carlos, Pangasinan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Binalatongan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Carlos is a city in the province of Pangasinan, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Under the name Binalatongan it was a leading town of the pre-colonial polity of Caboloan and the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-malong">Andrés Malong</a>, leader of the revolt of 1660. It was later renamed San Carlos and lies on the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agno-river">Agno River</a> plain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-carlos-pangasinan.jpg" alt="San Carlos, Pangasinan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P199 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Carlos_Pangasinan_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…civilian president José María Vargas, and the Federal War of 1859–1863, fought between Liberal federalists and Conservative centralists, was the bloodiest civil conflict of the century; the Liberal leader Ezequiel Zamora was killed at <b>San Carlos</b> in 1860, and the Treaty of Coche ended the war in 1863.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The people lived around the Lingayen Gulf and along the Agno River, with their centre at <b>Binalatongan</b>, later San Carlos.</blockquote><a href="/state/caboloan">Caboloan</a></li></ul></section>
